fr:mainmatter.fr:p:
Most fundamentally, a mathematician's job is to bring in novel and useful abstract concepts, capture them in their appropriate definitions or conjectures, and place them appropriately in the corpus of mathematical theory.
    Philosophers do this, often by inventing new technical terminology;
    scientists do this by defining new methods and measurements;
    artists do this but instead of technical terms and symbols, they use an artistic medium.

Most fundamentally, a mathematician's job is to bring in novel and useful abstract concepts, capture them in their appropriate definitions or conjectures, and place them appropriately in the corpus of mathematical theory.
    Philosophers do this, often by inventing new technical terminology;
    scientists do this by defining new methods and measurements;
    artists do this but instead of technical terms and symbols, they use an artistic medium.

However, software does much more than mathematical computation.
    As it gets higher in abstraction, software can become a social and financial intermediary between people, or even an agent of its own.
    The interface by which we use technology is software;
    the flow of a sales team's CRM is software;
    the media by which news gets reported and propagated is software.
    Software makes music, tells stories, implements art and design;
    it manages money and communication between people.

The primary point I'm making here with the (somewhat lengthy) discussion on smart contracts and their specifications is that software specification can range from highly technical and mathematical to highly intuitive and prose-based.
    The example of Dexter2 that I gave is probably still addressable with mathematical specifications;
    but at higher levels of abstraction, and in instances where software becomes more social and human-oriented, specifications get more difficult to write rigorously because there is no clearly defined, articulable theory with which one can use to derive the correctness of one's specification.
